There are also detractions to a tube heat exchanger, since it are not as efficient in heat
transfer as the plate and fin design. Shown in Figure 9 is a tube heat exchanger having
a cross flow arrangement. Because of its design, the tube heat exchanger is also
significantly larger that a shell and tube arrangement for a given heat dissipation rate .
Lastly, once the heat exchanger is defined within its design constraints, it is very difficult
to increase its size in order to attain a higher working capacity.
